  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": "When Should You Fix Damage Versus Offering a Price Reduction?",
  "author": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "HH Remodeling LLC"
  },
  "publisher": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "HH Remodeling LLC",
    "url": "http://coloradoremodeling.company"
  },
  "articleBody": "It's a common dilemma: do you spend money fixing damage before listing your property, or do you just adjust the price? The best approach often depends on the type of damage and its potential impact on buyers or renters. If it's a significant structural issue, an active leak, or an electrical hazard, you absolutely want to get that fixed. These aren't just cosmetic; they affect safety and the home's value, and almost any buyer's inspection will flag them. Trying to hide these can lead to bigger problems down the line, including negotiations falling through. For smaller, cosmetic issues, you might consider the cost of the repair versus the value it adds. If a repair costs more than twice the value it's likely to add, or if it's a project that costs more than 1% of the home's value but returns less than 75% at sale, it might be better to offer a credit or a slight price reduction. However, a lot of buyers prefer a move-in ready home, so sometimes fixing those minor cosmetic issues can still be beneficial. A trusted contractor can help you compare repair costs with potential resale value to make an informed decision.",
